It depends on role, but there are some
issues with this statement.

The main problem with rations is that the 
RNG can be quite stingy with them in the
early game.  Later they become very plentiful
indeed, especially once you start routinely
seeing soldiers.  But in the early game,
you can go thousands of turns without
seeing a food ration.  It's somewhat more
difficult to go thousands of turns without
eating.

One problem with the vast supply of monster 
corpses is they tend to arrive in spurts, 
usually in the form of live monsters that 
attack one after the other, without the 
common decency to stand by and leave you 
alone while you heal and/or eat their 
former comrades between matches.  The
ones that are both safe to eat and also
provide a decent amount of nutrition 
(e.g., orcs) are especially likely to 
occur in groups.  

The healing issue tends to force you back
up onto earlier dungeon levels where 
fewer of the monsters provide very many
calories, but also fewer of them can hit
you for more hit points per turn than
you can heal.  This tends to be a bigger
problem for physically-weak roles that
don't get a lot of hitpoints and don't
have the strength (or start with good
weapons) to kill things quickly.

Okay, so a group of orcs comes along,
and you eat them all, and now you're
satiated.  Naturally several more
high-nutrition safe-to-eat monsters
will come along while you're still
satiated.  As soon as you're NOT
satiated any longer, you start seeing
monsters that are either low-nutrition,
unsafe to eat, or both.  Yellow and
purple molds, for instance, are very 
common and very useless in this
situation.  This continues until you 
are weak from hunger or possibly already 
fainting, and THEN a group of orcs or 
elves or some jazz like that will come
along and kill you.

This never happens to characters that 
start with a +1 longsword.  They can
just make a beeline for Sokoban, mowing
down everything in their path.  It also
never happens to Monks.  But it does
happen to some of the other roles.

The other thing is, sometimes you don't
want to continue deeper into the dungeon
just yet.  Sometimes there's something 
dangerous down there that you just aren't
ready to deal with.  You need a better
weapon first, or some armor, or something.
It'd be nice to just hang out up here on
dungeon level two and kill wimpy stuff
for a while, maybe gain a level or two,
collect some daggers or armor or whatever...
but you can only do that if you aren't
hungry all the time.

